WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.198
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Thursday, November 26.

2
00:00:09.198 --> 00:00:25.448
Three things today: Kim Jong Un inspects nuclear sites and orders more tactical weapons, the Bank of Korea raises rates for the third time this year, and the Trump administration proposes new tariffs targeting fourteen economies including South Korea.

3
00:00:25.448 --> 00:00:39.950
On the first story: Kim made the inspections the same day that North Korea apparently ignored a direct outreach from President Trump at the APEC summit, and that tension goes to the heart of where diplomacy stands right now.

4
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:54.436
First, Kim Jong Un's nuclear inspection tour. On November 25, Kim visited three sites: a weapons-grade nuclear materials production base, a nuclear-powered equipment production base, and the Nuclear Weapons Institute.

5
00:00:54.436 --> 00:01:04.660
State media published photos of him examining warheads, what appear to be naval nuclear reactors, and rows of centrifuge cascades at two uranium enrichment facilities.

6
00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:15.865
From those sites, Kim ordered wider deployment of tactical nuclear weapons across multiple operational domains. He declared North Korea's nuclear status, in his words, "irreversible."

7
00:01:15.865 --> 00:01:30.314
His exact phrasing, in a statement carried by KCNA: "The nuclear forces of North Korea have been built up as a powerful treasured sword to protect the sovereignty and dignity of the state and people, and their status will never change."

8
00:01:30.314 --> 00:01:41.947
The timing is striking. N.K. News reports that Trump offered Kim a bilateral meeting at the APEC summit in Shenzhen. Kim ignored the offer. The inspections happened on the same day, and N.K.

9
00:01:41.947 --> 00:01:53.273
News separately reported that a ballistic missile engine test occurred during Trump's outreach. North Korea's state media made no mention of either Trump's overture or the missile test.

10
00:01:53.273 --> 00:02:08.273
Kim also explicitly rejected the idea of seeking recognition of North Korea's nuclear status from any country, which signals that Pyongyang is not positioning itself for the kind of arms-control-framed talks that some in Washington have floated.

11
00:02:08.273 --> 00:02:21.988
The bottom line on this story: Kim inspected nuclear warhead and enrichment facilities, ordered broader tactical nuclear deployment, and did all of it while publicly ignoring a direct diplomatic signal from the incoming U.S.

12
00:02:21.988 --> 00:02:22.600
president.

13
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:36.102
Second, the Bank of Korea raises rates again. The Bank of Korea raised its benchmark interest rate by 25 basis points to 3.25 percent on November 26. That is the third such increase in 2026.

14
00:02:36.102 --> 00:02:47.887
The Bank of Korea governor cited expansion in the artificial intelligence sector and inflationary pressure as the drivers. The central bank also upgraded its growth outlook for 2027.

15
00:02:47.887 --> 00:02:51.901
The rate now stands at its highest level in the current cycle.

16
00:02:51.901 --> 00:03:04.916
For Korea policy watchers, the rate decision matters because it reflects an economy performing strongly enough that the central bank is tightening, even as trade pressures from Washington are building.

17
00:03:04.916 --> 00:03:12.880
That dynamic is worth keeping in mind as the tariff story develops. The rate is now 3.25 percent, the third hike this year.

18
00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.353
Third, the Trump administration's new Section 301 tariff proposal.

19
00:03:18.353 --> 00:03:32.013
The administration has proposed new tariffs under Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974 against fourteen trading partners over what it calls industrial overcapacity. South Korea is on that list. But a senior U.S.

20
00:03:32.013 --> 00:03:45.738
official confirmed the proposed duties on South Korea will not exceed the 15 percent ceiling agreed under last year's bilateral trade deal. Japan, the European Union, and Taiwan also retain their 15 percent caps.

21
00:03:45.738 --> 00:03:50.270
China faces the highest rate, at 20 percent on top of earlier tariffs.

22
00:03:50.270 --> 00:04:03.865
The Korea Herald reports that Seoul has a second concern sitting alongside the overcapacity framing: South Korea's pending platform regulatory legislation. Twenty-two House Republicans sent a letter to the U.S.

23
00:04:03.865 --> 00:04:14.742
Trade Representative on the same issue, urging scrutiny of Seoul's proposed rules and citing potential harm to U.S. technology companies operating in the Korean market.

24
00:04:14.742 --> 00:04:27.690
That letter connects directly to the third story you need to watch from Seoul this week. The cap holds at 15 percent for South Korea, but digital regulation is now a live irritant in the relationship.

25
00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:41.105
Now to Pyongyang. The one article at the top of North Korea's state media today is not about Kim's nuclear inspection, which tells you something about how KCNA is managing its messaging. Daily N.K.

26
00:04:41.105 --> 00:04:54.685
reports that residents of Kimchaek in North Hamgyong Province faced continuous labor mobilization throughout 2026, working state construction projects seven days a week and taking on low-paying side jobs to afford food.

27
00:04:54.685 --> 00:05:03.924
As for Kim's inspection, state media led with his visit to the warhead and enrichment facilities and his order for wider tactical nuclear deployment.

28
00:05:03.924 --> 00:05:10.434
KCNA said nothing about Trump's APEC outreach or the ballistic missile engine test reported by N.K. News.

29
00:05:10.434 --> 00:05:27.610
The Workers' Party cell secretaries conference, attended by senior officials Jo Yong Won, Jong Kyong Thaek, and Ju Chang Il, reinforced ideological control, with Kim warning in a letter to the meeting that hostile forces would escalate their schemes against North Korean youth.

30
00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:30.656
The rest of the news, quickly.

31
00:05:30.656 --> 00:05:42.410
North Korea expelled the head of the Swiss diplomatic mission in Pyongyang, declaring Sylvain Astier persona non grata over what Pyongyang described as sanctions related to a children's camp.

32
00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:50.472
Switzerland said it "regrets" the decision. The closure removes one of the very few diplomatic presences the country had permitted.

33
00:05:50.472 --> 00:06:03.887
Russia's FSB and North Korea's State Intelligence Bureau signed a classified information pact in March, covering Moscow's highest state secrecy tier. The agreement was posted quietly on a Russian legal portal in April.

34
00:06:03.887 --> 00:06:14.348
Separately, Ukraine's military intelligence directorate says North Korea paused deliveries of KN-23 missiles and artillery to Russia amid a dispute that began in October.

35
00:06:14.348 --> 00:06:18.656
Russia earlier settled a currency dispute by paying Pyongyang in fuel.

36
00:06:18.656 --> 00:06:37.241
South Korea's Foreign Minister Cho Hyun spoke by phone with Iranian Foreign Minister Seyyed Abbas Araghchi on November 26, explaining that Seoul's planned expansion of the Cheonghae naval unit's mission is aimed at protecting South Korean nationals and economic interests, not participation in the war.

37
00:06:37.241 --> 00:06:49.425
Cho requested Iranian attention to the safety of South Korean nationals. His post-call statement described it as the tenth direct communication with Araghchi over approximately one and a half years.

38
00:06:49.425 --> 00:07:02.533
Russian aircraft that entered South Korea's Korea Air Defense Identification Zone on November 24 near Ulleung Island have been identified as Tu-160 supersonic strategic bombers capable of carrying nuclear weapons.

39
00:07:02.533 --> 00:07:12.195
Footage released by Russia's Defense Ministry also captured what appeared to be a Su-57 stealth fighter, though Seoul's Defense Ministry denied its presence.

40
00:07:12.195 --> 00:07:20.256
Defense experts said the Tu-160 exercise appeared aimed at countering U.S. Pacific forces and establishing access to Arctic routes.

41
00:07:20.256 --> 00:07:28.933
On personnel: President Lee approved confirmation hearing requests for four ministers and the National Intelligence Service director nominee.

42
00:07:28.933 --> 00:07:40.810
Former Democratic Party floor leader Hong Ik-pyo has been nominated as Unification Minister and told reporters he will pursue incremental inter-Korean engagement rather than high-level summits.

43
00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:51.272
Former Justice Minister Kang Kum-sil is nominated to lead the National Intelligence Service. Democratic Party Representative Han Jun-ho is nominated as Interior Minister.

44
00:07:51.272 --> 00:07:55.210
The National Assembly must complete the hearings within 20 days.

45
00:07:55.810 --> 00:08:10.009
The Democratic Party clarified that its impeachment push targets only Chief Justice Cho Hee-dae, responding to collective pushback from other judges. The party is building civil society support for the drive.

46
00:08:10.009 --> 00:08:22.706
The Supreme Court on November 26 upheld the acquittal of former President Yoon Suk Yeol on charges he perjured himself during the martial law trial of former Prime Minister Han Duck-soo.

47
00:08:22.706 --> 00:08:32.946
On the economy, Korean consumer goods exports reached a record 28.65 billion dollars from January 1 through November 19, up 21.5 percent year on year.

48
00:08:32.946 --> 00:08:40.319
The United States surpassed China as the top market for Korean consumer goods for the first time since 2004.

49
00:08:40.319 --> 00:08:59.570
A new Korea Economic Institute of America and YouGov survey finds that two-thirds of Americans view South Korea favorably, essentially unchanged since 2020, but a ten-point partisan gap has opened, with Republican favorability dropping from 76 percent in 2023 to 64 percent in 2024.

50
00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:10.970
A Gallup Korea poll taken November 3 to 5 put President Lee's approval at 41 percent, with the Democratic Party at 36 percent and the People Power Party at 28 percent.

51
00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:25.136
On Capitol Hill. Twenty-two House Republicans sent a letter to the U.S. Trade Representative urging scrutiny of South Korea's pending platform regulatory legislation, citing concerns about its impact on U.S.

52
00:09:25.136 --> 00:09:38.540
technology companies in the Korean market. That concern also appears in the Korea Herald's coverage of the Section 301 tariff proposal, where Seoul's digital rules are listed alongside overcapacity as a live issue.

53
00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:52.038
Beyond the region. South Korea is accelerating the selection of a new contractor for the Czech nuclear plant project after the Daewoo-Doosan consortium agreement was terminated on November 20.

54
00:09:52.038 --> 00:10:04.657
Korea Hydro and Nuclear Power is now directly managing some permitting work. The industry minister said keeping the promise to the Czech Republic is the first principle of all Team Korea decisions.

55
00:10:04.657 --> 00:10:17.980
Seoul and Riyadh have expanded their joint oil stockpiling arrangement. And OCBC Malaysia and South Korea's Hana Bank signed an agreement to jointly facilitate South Korean corporate investment into Malaysia.

56
00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:36.295
Finally, the markets. Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, closed at about 4,711, up about 0.3 percent. The dollar bought about 1,345 won. Brent crude fell sharply, down nearly 4 percent, to about 91 dollars a barrel.

57
00:10:36.295 --> 00:10:45.760
That's the Korea Daily Brief for Thursday, November 26. The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
